You can see here that you're defining the prop here, and then when you're accessing

00:06.760 --> 00:09.600
the slot prop here, it actually tells you what it is.

00:09.600 --> 00:14.520
And then for those of us that don't like to have a bunch of prefixes, you can actually

00:14.520 --> 00:18.440
destructure it, so it actually can just look like this as well.

00:18.440 --> 00:22.200
That's a standard JavaScript, nothing of you, that's just normal JavaScript.

00:22.200 --> 00:25.160
Okay, so you're probably thinking, okay, this is cool, but there's got to be some

00:25.160 --> 00:26.160
cons to this, right?

00:26.160 --> 00:27.160
And there is.

00:27.160 --> 00:30.000
What is called scope slot is because the context in which you're exposing the

00:30.000 --> 00:32.640
data is within a very specific scope.

00:32.640 --> 00:33.640
What does that scope?

00:33.640 --> 00:34.640
The slot.

00:34.640 --> 00:41.960
Meaning, we cannot now just go, hey, I want to do like constable count here, and then

00:41.960 --> 00:46.680
be like return count dot, like that's not possible.

00:46.680 --> 00:53.240
This prop is only exposed at the slot level, and so essentially, you're really only

00:53.240 --> 00:57.120
doing it for like, in my most of the time you're just doing it for

00:57.120 --> 01:00.000
display purposes and rearranging things.

01:00.000 --> 01:04.120
You're not trying to combine the logic and honestly, I'm not sure you would want to do

01:04.120 --> 01:07.200
that, you get really messy if you're able to do that.

01:07.200 --> 01:12.120
That's something to consider as far as how you can do that.

01:12.120 --> 01:18.680
Now, the other technique that I want to introduce you to is something called use slots.

01:18.680 --> 01:21.400
Why is use slots helpful?

01:21.400 --> 01:28.120
Let's say, for the footer, as you would expect, you have like an actual footer element

01:28.120 --> 01:36.800
that wraps this, and let's just say it has a bunch of my custom footer classes on it.

01:36.800 --> 01:43.880
And let's go ahead and actually eliminate any default content, because, well, sometimes

01:43.880 --> 01:46.400
sometimes there's no content.

01:46.400 --> 01:50.440
The problem with this is you might be able to imagine, is that when this is rendered,

01:50.440 --> 01:54.880
the footer element is still going to, the footer DOM element is still going to be rendered.

01:54.880 --> 01:59.360
Meaning you have essentially like orphaned HTML elements that really shouldn't be there

01:59.360 --> 02:01.120
up to begin with.

02:01.120 --> 02:08.680
And so what you can do is, if we import use slots, which is a helper method, it basically

02:08.680 --> 02:14.000
exposes what slots are being used inside of a given component at any time, like what's

02:14.000 --> 02:15.800
being passed in.

02:15.800 --> 02:18.720
So we can see here, use slots.

02:18.720 --> 02:30.360
And so what we can do is you can see here, I can go VIF slots dot footer.

02:30.360 --> 02:44.040
That should do the trick, and if we come over to the demo, oh, I have something in here.

02:44.040 --> 02:48.920
We can inspect.

02:48.920 --> 02:54.400
And if we take a look, that VIF has removed the footer element as you would want.

02:54.400 --> 02:59.560
And so this gives you additional control over how you want to actually use your slots,

02:59.560 --> 03:07.080
because before knowing this, your initial temptation might have been to just wrap everything

03:07.080 --> 03:08.360
in like a template block.

03:08.440 --> 03:12.640
And so you might have done something like this, and so you might have done like slot

03:12.640 --> 03:18.760
name is footer, and then you would have like thrown this inside of like thrown your

03:18.760 --> 03:21.400
footer element inside.

03:21.400 --> 03:28.720
But again, this only like, well, I mean, frankly, you're providing the slot content to

03:28.720 --> 03:29.520
render.

03:29.520 --> 03:32.840
So you kind of, it's not really what you can easily get out of it.

03:32.840 --> 03:36.760
And so this is why it's just a very clean way to basically check like, hey, does my slots

03:36.760 --> 03:42.160
contain like a footer slot, and so this is related to this name, by the way, that's

03:42.160 --> 03:44.360
what these two are connected.

03:44.360 --> 03:47.800
And so yeah, it's just a great way for you to programmatically get access to it.

03:47.800 --> 03:51.040
And then do any logic that you need to accordingly based on whatever slots are available

03:51.040 --> 03:52.040
to you.

03:52.040 --> 03:56.320
So as far as design patterns go, you'll notice that we focus a lot on composition in a lot

03:56.320 --> 03:57.640
techniques we talked about.

03:57.640 --> 04:00.440
In other words, like most of us when we start out with you, we learned about props,

04:00.440 --> 04:03.840
we learned about emits, and those are what we consider more configuration type design

04:03.840 --> 04:04.840
patterns.

04:04.840 --> 04:07.120
Very explicit about what API you're going to be using.

04:07.120 --> 04:09.960
I'm using this, and this, and I'm going to use it this way.

04:09.960 --> 04:13.520
But the problem with that is that it's very rigid, right?

04:13.520 --> 04:17.320
And so you can imagine, especially with there's an exercise that I do in the production

04:17.320 --> 04:21.960
great view course, where you take a base button and you add all these requirements to it.

04:21.960 --> 04:25.600
And if you do it, the props way, your props become a walking dictionary.

04:25.600 --> 04:27.480
And it's very, very difficult to navigate.

04:27.480 --> 04:32.920
It's one to if icon is left, then show this, and then if icon is right, show that it's

04:32.920 --> 04:34.080
pretty miserable.

04:34.080 --> 04:37.080
But composition, and I think one of the reasons why composition API has also taken

04:37.080 --> 04:40.760
off the way it has is because it allows you to really organically grow your components

04:40.760 --> 04:46.040
to a point where it does make sense to specialize them, whereas configuration, you specialize

04:46.040 --> 04:47.040
immediately.

04:47.040 --> 04:50.640
You're like, oh, I want a button that has a left icon, then you create a prop that says

04:50.640 --> 04:52.440
left icon equals this.

04:52.440 --> 04:56.680
Instead of like, here's a button, you can put stuff in it, use it.

04:56.680 --> 05:01.080
And then if over time you're like, oh, I noticed fifth, like 500 components have always

05:01.080 --> 05:04.080
used a left button icon with these styles.

05:04.080 --> 05:07.080
Then that's when it makes sense to be like, okay, we're going to create a base button,

05:07.080 --> 05:09.040
let's say left icon.

05:09.040 --> 05:10.640
And then that can refactor all that out.

05:10.640 --> 05:14.320
And then so what you get is what I like to do about data driven refactoring, rather than

05:14.320 --> 05:18.320
sort of like when you're doing props is easy to get into like optimizing what you think

05:18.320 --> 05:23.080
you might eventually need, composition kind of frees you of that, because you can totally

05:23.080 --> 05:26.920
expand to whatever you need, but at the same time you're not bound to those very rigid

05:26.920 --> 05:27.920
definitions upfront.

05:28.640 --> 05:31.640
So just something about is not that you should never do configuration.

05:31.640 --> 05:36.480
In fact, we saw in some of our base text base components that when you're certain

05:36.480 --> 05:40.400
things are, you absolutely want to be in their configuration is really helpful.

05:40.400 --> 05:43.640
But composition is the one I do try to get with the default to when they're designing

05:43.640 --> 05:46.640
their components is a more flexible and just easier to evolve.

05:46.640 --> 05:51.640
Would the most common use for scope slots be the past components into them, light a career

